WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.420
There's a lot to love about the Marvel Cinematic
Universe.

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:05.980
It's got action, adventure, and baby Groot!

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:11.080
That's what everyone adores about the MCU,
but there's one thing the sprawling, multi-billion-dollar

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:15.920
movie series hasn't gotten quite right: LGBTQ
community representation.

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:18.780
Many fans have called for Marvel to remedy
this.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:20.420
The proposed solution?

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:23.620
Simply include more LGBTQ characters in the
MCU!

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:27.980
And according to Marvel Studios president
Kevin Feige, that's exactly what the company

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:28.800
plans to do.

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:33.860
Speaking in an interview with The Playlist,
Feige revealed that Marvel Studios will introduce

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:38.789
at least two LGBTQ characters in the future,
one will be someone fans have never seen on

00:00:38.789 --> 00:00:42.960
the big screen before, and another will be
a character viewers are already familiar with.

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:46.880
The executive didn't mince words or skirt
around the topic when it was brought up.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:53.420
When asked if "a gay, bi, LGBTQ, out character
in the MCU" was in the works, Feige responded:

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:54.840
"Yeah, that's the answer.

00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.320
[…] Both ones you've seen and ones you haven't
seen."

00:00:57.580 --> 00:01:01.639
Though Feige didn't dish up any additional
details, keeping a tight lip on the characters'

00:01:01.639 --> 00:01:06.790
identities, it's widely assumed that Tessa
Thompson's Thor: Ragnarok hero Valkyrie is

00:01:06.790 --> 00:01:10.240
the LGBTQ character who has already graced
us with her presence.

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:14.700
The actress previously confirmed that Valkyrie
is bisexual, writing on Twitter back in October

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:16.189
of 2017: "She's bi.

00:01:16.189 --> 00:01:19.110
And yes, she cares very little about what
men think of her.

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:20.480
What a joy to play!"

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:22.380
"Asgard is danger.

00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:25.180
People are dying and we need to get back there,
I need YOUR help."

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:28.080
"Finished.

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:29.200
Bye."

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:33.800
Sadly, only part of that proclamation was
reflected in the final version of the movie.

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:37.700
Valkyrie certainly didn't care about men's
opinions, but the single scene that addressed

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:39.500
her sexuality was cut from the film.

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:44.090
After the devastating events of Avengers:
Infinity War, we aren't sure whether Valkyrie

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:45.260
is actually alive.

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:49.620
Still, fans have their fingers crossed that
she's a-okay and will be out and proud in

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:53.850
an upcoming Marvel movie, especially since
that would reflect the character's modern

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:55.260
day comic book identity.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:02:00.880
As for who the brand-new LGBTQ character is
and where they may first appear, the possibilities

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:01.660
are endless.

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:05.719
Will Brie Larson's Carol Danvers have a lady
love in Captain Marvel?

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:11.319
Or could Spider-Man: Far From Home introduce
an LGBTQ teen vigilante like America Chavez

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:13.060
to banter by Spidey's side?

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:17.140
Your guess is as good as ours, but when it
comes down to it, we're just excited that

00:02:17.140 --> 00:02:21.420
Marvel is taking any sort of initiative to
make its movie universe more inclusive.

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:22.720
"It's about time."

